The Aztec Sun Stone is a carved basalt disk nearly four meters across and
weighing roughly twenty-four tonnes. At its center is a face, surrounded by
concentric rings of glyphs recording days, cosmic ages, and the directions, a
calendar and a cosmos in one circular monument.

## Five suns

The stone records the Mexica belief in successive world-ages, or "suns," each
created and then destroyed. The central face is commonly read as the present
sun, flanked by the symbols of the ages that came before.

## A circle of time

Like a mandala, the Sun Stone organizes everything around a center and reads
outward in rings. Here the rings are time itself: days, months, and ages
arranged around the present moment.

